Oh - oh, yes, I've seen him.\" Percy says. [673] \"I agree.\" Ren says casually. \"A clever young man, isn't he?\" [674] \"Yes, yes.\" [675] The inspector is somewhat agitated. \"Ren, actually, what we're dealing with is quite mad. I haven't taken any fees. This is the craziest thing you've ever heard of. For old times' sake, I have to do something about it.\" [676] \"Not worth envying,\" the old gentleman couldn't help murmuring, \"I think we should go to the museum right away. Inspector, you described the broken glass cabinet in the Saxon room, and I'd like to check out what's inside.\" [677] \"Oh!\" Pechin exclaimed, \"Did I miss something?\" [678] \"Just a guess,\" Mr. Lerne said thoughtfully. \"I'm sure there's nothing. Let's go, Dromio is waiting for us downstairs.\" [679] They found Dr. Allonsio Jotte talking to a tall, thin man with long limbs and wearing strange foreign clothes. He had a typical British narrow face and sharp eyes, a brown, unframed spectacles perched on the bridge of his nose, a black silk ribbon attached to the frame, which wrapped around his neck. His bony face was clean-shaven, and his well-groomed beard gave him the appearance of a Renaissance scholar. His tone was calm and confident, and his refined accent revealed that he was an educated Englishman. He appeared to be around fifty years old. Dr. Jotte introduced him as Dr. Hamnett Sedra, the future curator, who had arrived on a British ship that morning. [680] \"Mr. Lerne!\" he exclaimed. \"It's an honor to meet you. Ever since I saw your performance of 'Othello' in London twenty years ago, I've wanted to meet you. And then, your scholarly articles on Shakespeare in 'The Review'\u2026\"\n[681] \"You're too kind,\" the old man hastened to say, \"I'm only half-knowledgeable in literature. I believe Dr. Quart told you about the strange incident that occurred here before you arrived?\" [682] Dr. Sedley seemed unaware of the matter. \"I'm sorry, what?\" [683] \"Oh, a trivial matter,\" Dr. Quart reluctantly said, stroking his goat's beard, \"Mr. Lenoir, I'm surprised you take this so seriously.\" [684] \"The circumstances seem quite odd and superficial, Dr. Sedley,\" Mr. Lenoir whispered, his bright eyes darting from Dr. Quart to Dr. Sedley and back again, \"Dr. Sedley, on Monday\u2014two days ago, someone obviously infiltrated the museum and clearly disturbed a cabinet in this new room.\" [685] \"Really?\" Dr. Sedley asked. [686] \"Nothing serious.\" The curator said impatiently, \"He didn't take anything, and that's the most important thing.\" [687] \"I suppose so.\" The British man smiled in agreement. [688] \"Can I interrupt this academic dispute?\" The old gentleman said, \"Can I suggest re-examining the evidence itself? Or would you all rather...\"\n[689] Dr. Jotter nodded. The British man said, \"I believe I'm quite familiar with Dr. Jotter by now. I have nothing more important to do than to look at this broken glass cabinet.\" [690] \"Oh, yes, yes.\" The curator furrowed his brow, \"Since you want to see it, of course, you can.\" [691] They walked through the empty general reading room. Percyns glanced around, a little disappointed: where was Gordon Roeve? They entered the Saxon room. The glass that had been broken yesterday had been replaced, and the cabinet was as spotless and gleaming as the rest of the room. [692] \"The glazier fixed it yesterday,\" Dr. Jott challenged the sergeant, \"I can assure you he was never alone. I stood right next to him until he finished the job.\" [693] The sergeant snorted. [694] Mr. Lane and Dr. Sedra looked at the glass curiously, their eyes shining with admiration. [695] \"Jagger's.\" Dr. Sedra said gently, \"Mr. Lane, that's remarkable. Dr. Jott, you say this room is new, and these artifacts are recent donations?\" [696] \"Yes. The collections in this room were bequeathed to Britain by the collector Samuel Saxon. When the museum reopens, they will of course be on display.\" [697] \"Oh, of course. I remember 